{"name":"Chopsticks","hex":"#e0d1b8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Chopsticks","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A simple waltz, written in 1877 by Arthur de Lulli, that serves as a two-finger exercise for beginner piano players."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Chopsticks"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chopsticks","blocks":[{"text":"Chopsticks are shaped pairs of equal-length sticks that have been used as kitchen and eating utensils in most countries of the Sinosphere for over three millennia. They are held in the dominant hand, secured by fingers, and wielded as extensions of the hand, to pick up food."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Many-chopsticks.jpg/330px-Many-chopsticks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chopsticks"},{"title":"Chopsticks (hand game)","blocks":[{"text":"Chopsticks is a hand game for two or more players, in which players extend a number of fingers from each hand and transfer those scores by taking turns tapping one hand against another. Chopsticks is an example of a combinatorial game, and is solved in the sense that, with perfect play, an optimal strategy from any point is known."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Hands_as2_and_3.svg/330px-Hands_as2_and_3.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chopsticks_(hand_game)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/KOCIS_Korean_meal_table_%284553953910%29.jpg/330px-KOCIS_Korean_meal_table_%284553953910%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/KOCIS_Korean_meal_table_%284553953910%29.jpg/330px-KOCIS_Korean_meal_table_%284553953910%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_c